\section{Distinct-3 Results}
\label{app:distinct3}

Table~\ref{tab:distinct3_drift} reports distinct-3 drift across all contamination levels.
Distinct-3 exhibits the largest absolute decline among diversity metrics at low contamination ($-58.9{\pm}0.9\%$ at $\alpha{=}0.25$) but attenuates at higher levels ($-30.5\%$ at $\alpha{=}1.0$), consistent with the diversity--calibration dissociation discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Distinct-3 drift (\%, generation~0$\to$10) across contamination levels. $\alpha{=}0.00$, $\alpha{=}0.25$, $\alpha{=}0.75$: 3-seed mean${\pm}$std; $\alpha{=}1.0$: 4-seed mean${\pm}$std; $\alpha{=}0.50$: single seed.}
\label{tab:distinct3_drift}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
$\alpha$ & Distinct-3 drift (\%) \\
\midrule
0.00 & $-4.4{\pm}0.5$ \\
0.25 & $-58.9{\pm}0.9$ \\
0.50 & $-52.4$ \\
0.75 & $-38.6{\pm}1.3$ \\
1.00 & $-30.5{\pm}4.0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Lead-lag analysis.}
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, token entropy Granger-precedes distinct-3 at the same consensus level as distinct-1 and distinct-2: $K{\geq}4/5$ methods, $S{\geq}3/4$ seeds (Tier~3).
However, the optimal lag is less stable than for distinct-1/2: at max\_lag${=}7$, the lag standard deviation across seeds exceeds 1.5 generations, compared to $<0.5$ for distinct-1/2.
This instability reflects bimodal lag distributions (modes at lag~1 and lag~3--4), suggesting that distinct-3 captures two temporally distinct diversity loss mechanisms: rapid n-gram homogenization (lag~1) and slower distributional narrowing (lag~3--4).

\paragraph{Sensitivity to sequence length.}
Higher-order $n$-gram diversity metrics are more sensitive to generation length and random sampling variability.
With our 512-token generation length, distinct-3 has a smaller effective denominator (number of possible unique trigrams observed) than distinct-1 or distinct-2, amplifying stochastic fluctuations.
This partially explains the higher lag instability: the signal-to-noise ratio for temporal precedence is lower for distinct-3, requiring more observations (longer $T$ or more seeds) to achieve stable estimates.
For this reason, we report distinct-3 in this appendix rather than in the main text, where distinct-1 and distinct-2 provide cleaner evidence of the entropy-leads-diversity pattern.


\section{Observation Window Sensitivity}
\label{app:window_sensitivity}

Table~\ref{tab:window_sensitivity} reports how the Tier~3 consensus classification ($K{\geq}4/5$ methods) varies as a function of the minimum observation window $T_{\min}$, where analysis uses only generations $0$ through $T_{\min}$.
The consensus classification stabilizes at $T_{\min}{\geq}5$ (6 data points), well below our full observation window of $T{=}10$: all six effect-size reference pairs (token entropy and ECE $\to$ distinct-1, distinct-2, distinct-3) reach $K{\geq}4/5$ agreement at every window length from $T_{\min}{=}5$ through $T_{\min}{=}10$, with no spurious pairs (canary$\to$perplexity) admitted.

Toda--Yamamoto is the only method exhibiting window-dependent sensitivity (note: TY directional assignment is under verification; these counts are retained for completeness): TY consensus stabilizes at $T_{\min}{\geq}6$, slightly later than the primary methods, reflecting the additional degrees of freedom consumed by the augmentation order $d_{\max}$.
This does not affect the consensus classification because the remaining four methods provide stable $K{\geq}4/5$ agreement independently of Toda--Yamamoto's vote.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Granger precedence is not causation.}
Our claim is scoped to temporal predictive improvement~\cite{granger1969investigating,shojaie2022granger}; bivariate tests cannot exclude confounders, and our cluster-adjusted FPR CI ($[0\%, 41\%]$; \S\ref{sec:limitations}) means true Type~I error may be non-negligible.

\paragraph{Non-confirming cells.}
Five of twenty phase-diagram cells show $0/8$ despite lying above the theoretical detection boundary: four are sync-dip cells ($\alpha{=}0.60$, $T{=}10$--$20$) and one is a boundary cell ($T{=}10$, $\alpha{=}0.45$).
The sync-dip dissolves by $T{=}25$ ($5/8$), confirming it is transient; its mechanism remains open---one hypothesis is synchronization between the replacement ratio and the model's internal mode dynamics.
Excluding these five cells, the remaining 15/15 match theory (LOO-CV MAE${=}0.037$).

\paragraph{Metric-specific ceiling.}
Observed detection counts never exceed $6/8$: the 2/8 non-detected pairs involve MAUVE, whose floor saturation ($\mu{=}0.029$) eliminates the temporal gradient Granger precedence requires (\S\ref{sec:t20}); practitioners should prefer monotonically degrading metrics (e.g., distinct-$n$).

\paragraph{Cross-architecture failure modes.}
Five of fifteen conditions do not confirm forward detection (\S\ref{sec:generalization}): precision sensitivity (GPT-2 bf16: 0/8 vs fp32 6/8), bidirectional confounding (GPT-2 s43: reverse 6/8), seed dependence (Pythia-1.4B: s42 2/8 vs s43 6/8), and architecture-specific decoupling (SmolLM3-3B entropy 0/4, violating mode amplification; Proposition~\ref{thm:entropy_leads}).
Phase-diagram replication at 1.4B confirms qualitatively preserved detection despite count attenuation (4--5/8; Appendix~\ref{app:scale_14b}).

\paragraph{Entropy-diversity coupling.}
The entropy-diversity lag is structural: distinct-$n$ requires token mass to cross threshold~$\varepsilon$ ($\gamma$-margin; Proposition~\ref{prop:div_lag}), producing the ${\geq}1$-generation lag in 14/15 experiments; PermG's directional asymmetry (6/8 forward, 0/8 reverse) argues against a sensitivity artifact.
However, directionality is domain-dependent: WikiText yields exclusively forward significance, while C4 produces reverse 7/8 at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Longer $T$ lowers $\alpha_c$ sub-linearly (\S\ref{sec:theory}); PermG requires fp32 (bf16: 0/8 vs 6/8 in GPT-2).
Triggered cessation on entropy (optimal canary; 14/15 cascade) reduces peak perplexity by 58\% (\S\ref{sec:intervention}), bridging detection to action.

